#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "a7899e04-8be9-597a-a9b6-111201ebad2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8607" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ on the line segment $\\overline {AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x, y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a =  \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ (9-{2}^{2}-{1}^{2})-(9-{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2x,-2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =⟨-2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =-4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{1-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1,c_2\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $4c_1 + 2c_2 = 5,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{c}_{1}+2(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}) & =5 \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{1} & =5 \\\\ 5{c}_{1} & =5 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find $c_2 = \\dfrac{c_1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $\\left(1, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "2b224972-6a43-5691-9e46-abff577311d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8608" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x, y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b= \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3,0)-f(0,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3,0⟩-⟨0,0,1⟩) \\\\ 7-(-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,1,-2z⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =4{c}_{1}+3+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{3}=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx = 2t , \\quad y= 3t, \\quad z = 1-t, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c= \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 = 2t , \\quad c_2 = 3t , \\quad c_3 = 1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $4c_1 + 2c_3 = 5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2t)+2(1-t) & =5 \\\\ 8t+2-2t & =5 \\\\ 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of $c_1,$ $c_2,$ and $c_3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})=1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(1, \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "0368b61d-2d85-58af-9af0-ec09edf877b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8693" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where $\\mathbf a=\\langle 5,3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 4,2\\rangle$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(\\mathbf a)= 5$ and $f(\\mathbf b)=6,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c?$\n\nI. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = -1$\n\nII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = 1$\n\nIII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) +f_y(\\mathbf c) = 2$" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 6-5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨4,2⟩-⟨5,3⟩) \\\\ 1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 1 & =-{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\nf_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) =-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191e0857-4af3-54f7-b915-63c472d131e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)-5{f}_{y}(c)=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447488} {:answer/id #uuid "d20d4356-f0e9-5813-aee5-0e7286bae160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c)=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447578} {:answer/id #uuid "d41cdbde-f333-5a13-81de-47a16ab4d8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447579} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1348cc-1582-58d7-82fc-35147b2e1d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447592} {:answer/id #uuid "fac1e092-e375-5c3b-aaff-d23ceaeabfaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d20d4356-f0e9-5813-aee5-0e7286bae160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c)=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447578} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea6eed03-9831-52dd-806d-2cce8dda7014" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04ad605f-8ccb-55ea-8b18-2d7ae6e4a34a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105744" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨3,-1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=4$ and $f(b)=-2,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ -2-4 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨1,4⟩-⟨3,-1⟩) \\\\ -6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,5⟩ \\\\ -6 & =-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e91ee75-c53a-5920-9a45-5ccb0a2e7cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{f}_{x}(c)+7{f}_{y}(c)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564047} {:answer/id #uuid "3515ef3b-1145-5a2c-83ec-ef4b995ee066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c)=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564055} {:answer/id #uuid "7d424855-c52c-5f97-848a-81dd93164aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564162} {:answer/id #uuid "bc0ddb38-2274-5275-a53e-375a42e5487d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-7{f}_{y}(c)=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564257} {:answer/id #uuid "ed84c972-9d23-59fb-87df-f4c078367fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{f}_{x}(c)+7{f}_{y}(c)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc0ddb38-2274-5275-a53e-375a42e5487d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-7{f}_{y}(c)=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564257} :answer-field/id #uuid "63dbf298-8043-509c-939e-873fdfa9afc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a261cf1f-d1e0-58d8-b832-e9ddda3c5b83" :question/math-academy-id "q-105745" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨2,5⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-2⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=-3$ and $f(b)=5,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 5-(-3) & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-2⟩-⟨2,5⟩) \\\\ 8 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-7⟩ \\\\ 8 & ={f}_{x}(c)-7{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77ea2481-3641-5d62-90a6-8b6caf3cf8c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564152} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbff4da-22a8-5978-8ab8-a17622d4fde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+{f}_{z}(c)=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564322} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c4e6f4-8acc-5247-8b67-ba9dc37255f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564326} {:answer/id #uuid "e85f9d44-f329-5e20-aaa4-f71caa80370d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564335} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fb1752-c3b8-5541-93ef-2714c93c51b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e85f9d44-f329-5e20-aaa4-f71caa80370d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564335} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e1c7f39-4e9f-5c82-b6fa-abd61898eb6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "602e236f-f7ee-52d9-b1ca-e9d90f7ae370" :question/math-academy-id "q-105765" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨2,-1,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-1,1⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=6$ and $f(b)=2,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 2-6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-1,1⟩-⟨2,-1,3⟩) \\\\ -4 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\ -4 & ={f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that ${f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c)=-4.$ *"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "448a0750-9b44-5659-83b7-923153eddba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564084} {:answer/id #uuid "a7dcc3a0-efb9-5f2d-8dcb-78d2815d4b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564226} {:answer/id #uuid "c19232e5-f92c-5f53-ab11-91b76aef364a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564267} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fc269a-8a0c-5e6f-b0a6-fbc93fcbbf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564343} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2ef189-5218-5f4d-b4b4-6ce38062ef65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "448a0750-9b44-5659-83b7-923153eddba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564084} :answer-field/id #uuid "c781cea5-efe5-5962-aeb0-616e30494f29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90802828-69ad-5b22-b55e-e0d3346dd2ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-106066" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=xy$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=xy$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ 2(4)-0(0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =2{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1} \\\\ 4 & ={c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{4-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $2{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}=8,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{1} & =4 \\\\ 4{c}_{1} & =4 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=2{c}_{1}=2.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1be1a0af-86dc-5ce9-b9dd-45a2a74d1d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564005} {:answer/id #uuid "217dd2de-918f-5112-b69d-bfbe04aa4177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564016} {:answer/id #uuid "3731b121-3d08-5491-b0f5-428c1d0c462f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2},6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564060} {:answer/id #uuid "b81437b6-492a-5768-8149-02555c33e13b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564252} {:answer/id #uuid "e908e1ac-36c7-569f-adae-97102cdf51d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "217dd2de-918f-5112-b69d-bfbe04aa4177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564016} :answer-field/id #uuid "de865212-4382-5303-8e7d-34adf3e8bc14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6547ba2d-be83-5b17-a5ce-a14f2d6a7139" :question/math-academy-id "q-106068" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨3,7⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨3,7⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,7)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,7⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ ({7}^{2}-{3}^{2})-({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2x,2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}39 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =⟨-2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =-6{c}_{1}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 13 & =-2{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{7-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{6} & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y-1 & =2x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $-2{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}=13,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+4(2{c}_{1}+1) & =13 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{1}+4 & =13 \\\\ 6{c}_{1} & =9 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=2{c}_{1}+1=4.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b2b739-da94-553c-b42f-0bb5d830c78c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)+2{f}_{z}(c)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563981} {:answer/id #uuid "1794b49b-1f1d-51f2-bedf-3e99b67fa251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564002} {:answer/id #uuid "261c21c2-d16a-58fe-a2ee-2828cc3a3b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{f}_{x}(c)-3{f}_{y}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564024} {:answer/id #uuid "754bba4a-fb4d-5db6-b459-3248551d0a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)+{f}_{z}(c)=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564146} {:answer/id #uuid "e263679e-0346-5705-aeb7-b8a5c54c9770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)+3{f}_{z}(c)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "754bba4a-fb4d-5db6-b459-3248551d0a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)+{f}_{z}(c)=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564146} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dc25338-e3f2-565d-8f71-40061dd4364a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17eec8d8-70be-5d2a-8c5e-928ea708ee3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130834" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨1,0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨0,2,3⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=-2$ and $f(b)=5,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 5-(-2) & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨0,2,3⟩-⟨1,0,2⟩) \\\\ 7 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 7 & =-{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)+{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that ${f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=-7.$ *"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016736eb-caa6-5d48-8fbf-47af4434c9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447478} {:answer/id #uuid "0afea1a8-95a6-556d-a3e7-0e440998ec23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)-4{f}_{y}(c)-3{f}_{z}(c)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447485} {:answer/id #uuid "20aa54b1-4024-5833-be7a-094831fe92d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)+{f}_{z}(c)=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447492} {:answer/id #uuid "246ed905-e97b-5fe0-ab91-95d7dda0c05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447494} {:answer/id #uuid "a04de735-f8d9-5805-bcc1-3800999914a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)-3{f}_{y}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "246ed905-e97b-5fe0-ab91-95d7dda0c05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447494} :answer-field/id #uuid "97502e7b-e0c1-5dff-b994-54b47731e1e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c15a8036-6858-5a29-a56c-8e96a5ccf287" :question/math-academy-id "q-130836" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨-1,-1,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,0,-2⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=5$ and $f(b)=-1,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ -1-5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,0,-2⟩-⟨-1,-1,3⟩) \\\\ -6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨4,1,-5⟩ \\\\ -6 & =4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=-6.$ *"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0222ab38-0a5d-5644-b548-b49f314a6f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447480} {:answer/id #uuid "6f049334-5ef6-5847-8455-946eb960be10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447534} {:answer/id #uuid "9718bb12-ffd6-5092-abb0-5cf104f4bf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{8}{3},\\frac{11}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447560} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a5f9b0-a9d6-5235-859a-a70242d8865e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447567} {:answer/id #uuid "df2c5b96-7e46-538a-b432-978f7151418c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{19}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6a5f9b0-a9d6-5235-859a-a70242d8865e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447567} :answer-field/id #uuid "301da0d6-3394-5ca9-822c-794b43878a03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cde771f2-0ae1-5186-919d-63d0f22b095d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36826" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,4)-f(0,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,4⟩-⟨0,3⟩) \\\\ 3(4)^{2}+3(4)+2(3)^{2}-(3(3)^{2}+0(3)+2(0)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x+y,x+6y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =12{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\ 51 & =13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-3}{4-3} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}=51,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13{c}_{1}+9(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3) & =51 \\\\ 13{c}_{1}+3{c}_{1}+27 & =51 \\\\ 16{c}_{1} & =24 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7a5f5b-69d3-59c5-9fe5-d1c3092257a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484837} {:answer/id #uuid "1838f568-f154-5028-8a40-70828f862bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484865} {:answer/id #uuid "31c22ae3-b2f9-5039-88b3-e917ad61579f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484919} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f935b5-6dfe-5bcd-8f1a-c9dd6711bf82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485207} {:answer/id #uuid "d9857530-796f-58fe-9f92-b5bb11123e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{11}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1838f568-f154-5028-8a40-70828f862bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484865} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcf7b736-691e-5d1f-a95e-1aa8428199b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6c8dce9-3855-5452-ba32-6512e799f65b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36836" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3)-f(0,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3⟩-⟨0,2⟩) \\\\ (5(3)^{2}-2(2))-(5(2)^{2}-2(0)) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2,10y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =-4+10{c}_{2} \\\\ 10{c}_{2} & =25 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3-2} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & =2y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2{c}_{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2},$ we obtain that ${c}_{1}=2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-4=1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(1,\\frac{5}{2}).$"}]}
